administration has agreed to hand control of California guard troops to the state.","captionComponent":{"text":"National Guard soldiers at the Federal Building in Los Angeles in June. The Trump administration has agreed to hand control of California guard troops to the state."},"identifier":"_anf-photo-1","imageIdentifier":"af45279719aa61edab437fdda2d25757","layout":"figure-image","role":"photo","type":"scalable_image"},{"components":[{"conditional":[{"conditions":[{"maxSpecVersion":"1.7"}],"hidden":true}],"hidden":false,"identifier":"_anf-divider-2","layout":"figcaption-colorline","role":"divider","stroke":{"color":"#10B792","width":"1.0pt"},"type":"line"},{"components":[{"identifier":"_anf-caption-2","layout":"figcaption-caption-listmode","role":"caption","text":"Gabrielle Lurie, S.F. Chronicle","textStyle":"figcaption-credit-listmode","type":"text"},{"identifier":"_anf-container-5","layout":"ComponentLayout-1","role":"container","type":"container"},{"identifier":"_anf-caption-1","layout":"figcaption-caption-listmode","role":"caption","text":"National Guard soldiers at the Federal Building in Los Angeles in June. The Trump administration has agreed to hand control of California guard troops to the state.","textStyle":"figcaption-caption-listmode","type":"text"}],"identifier":"_anf-container-4","layout":"_defaultComponentLayout","role":"container","type":"container"}],"contentDisplay":{"type":"horizontal_stack"},"identifier":"_anf-container-3","layout":"figcaption","role":"container","type":"container"}],"identifier":"_anf-container-2","layout":"figure","role":"container","type":"container"},{"additions":[{"URL":"https://x.com/TheCalGuard","analytics":{"linkType":"web"},"range":{"length":12,"start":2119},"type":"link"},{"URL":"https://x.com/GovPressOffice/status/2006406409982988417?s=20","analytics":{"linkType":"web"},"range":{"length":12,"start":2159},"type":"link"},{"URL":"https://x.com/TheCalGuard","analytics":{"linkType":"web"},"range":{"length":12,"start":2369},"type":"link"},{"URL":"https://apple.news/PgIDLYtvDqBH-mQIvOwYiwD","analytics":{"linkType":"web"},"range":{"length":14,"start":3108},"type":"link"}],"identifier":"body1","layout":"body-layout","role":"body","text":"President Donald Trump has agreed to return control of California’s National Guard to the state, at least for now, in the wake of the Supreme Court’s refusal to allow him to send National Guard troops from other states to Chicago.\n\nThe Trump administration, with court approval, took over the state’s National Guard in June and sent 4,000 of its soldiers, along with 700 U.S. Marines, to Los Angeles to protect immigration officers and federal property from protesters of immigration workplace raids. But U.S. District Judge Charles Breyer ruled in September that the guard troops had illegally taken part in law enforcement actions, and on Dec. 10 he ordered the Trump administration to end federalization of the guard and return control to California Gov. Gavin Newsom.\n\nThe 9th U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals quickly issued a stay of Breyer’s ruling, allowing Trump to retain authority over the troops — though not to deploy them to Los Angeles or other cities — while the legality of his takeover was debated in court. But on Tuesday, Trump’s Justice Department told the appeals court it was no longer seeking a stay.\n\n“In light of the Supreme Court’s decision in Trump v. Illinois,” and “without prejudice as to any other arguments” supporting federalization of the National Guard, administration officials “do not oppose lifting of the partial administrative stay and hereby respectfully withdraw their motion for a stay pending appeal,” Justice Department attorney J. Kain Day told the court.\n\nIn other words, they’ll still argue that they are entitled to take control of a state’s National Guard and send its troops to city streets, but California can have them back for now.\n\nTrump demobilized most of the California National Guard troops he sent to Los Angeles and has tried to send some of them on similar missions to Portland, Ore., and Chicago, but has been blocked by court orders.\n\nThe turnaround in control of the California troops will take effect once the appeals court issues an order. It was welcomed Wednesday by Brandon Richards, a spokesperson for Newsom.\n\n“We’ve long known the federalization of @TheCalGuard is illegal,” Richards said in a posting on X. “This admission by Trump and his occult cabinet members means this illegal intimidation tactic will finally come to an end. We look forward to the Ninth Circuit officially ruling — returning @TheCalGuard to state service.”\n\nState Attorney General Rob Bonta said California’s guard troops “have been used as political pawns by a President desperate to be king.”\n\n“Now, in the face of a stinging rebuke by the U.S. Supreme Court, the Trump Administration is backing away from its efforts to federalize and deploy California National Guard troops,” Bonta said in a statement.\n\nThe Supreme Court’s 6-3 conservative majority has generally supported Trump’s claims of expanded executive authority, shielding him from criminal prosecution for official actions and signaling that it will allow him to fire members of independent federal agencies such as the Federal Trade Commission and the National Labor Relations Board.\n\nBut the court ruled Dec. 23 that Trump lacked authority under federal law to send National Guard troops from other states to Chicago, one of several Democratic-led cities where the president has alleged, with little evidence, that crime and violence are out of control.\n\nThe court’s majority said the president had failed to satisfy the longtime standards of federal law, which allow a president to order a state’s National Guard into active service only if the president is “unable with the regular forces to execute the laws of the United States.”\n\nThose “regular forces” are military troops, the court said, and under the Posse Comitatus Act of 1878 — the same federal law cited by Breyer in his ruling in September — U.S. military troops cannot be used to enforce criminal laws. The ruling was unsigned, but only three of the nine justices — Samuel Alito, Clarence Thomas and Neil Gorsuch — publicly dissented.","type":"text"}],"hints":{"conditionals":{"autoplacement":false,"componentLayouts":true,"componentStyles":false,"componentTextStyles":true,"components":true,"documentStyle":false,"textStyles":false},"conditions":{"_anf_testing":false,"app":false,"configurationKey":false,"experimentVariant":false,"following":false,"horizontalSizeClass":false,"maxColumns":false,"maxContentSizeCategory":false,"maxSpecVersion":true,"maxViewportAspectRatio":false,"maxViewportWidth":false,"minColumns":false,"minContentSizeCategory":false,"minSpecVersion":false,"minViewportAspectRatio":false,"minViewportWidth":true,"newsletter":false,"offerIdentifier":false,"platform":false,"preferredColorScheme":false,"subscriptionActivationEligibility":false,"subscriptionStatus":false,"upsellScenario":false,"verticalSizeClass":false,"viewLocation":false}},"identifier":"article-21270101","language":"en","layout":{"columns":20,"gutter":20,"margin":60,"width":1200},"metadata":{"canonicalURL":"https://www.sfchronicle.com/politics/article/trump-return-control-california-national-guard-21270101.php","thumbnailImageIdentifier":"af45279719aa61edab437fdda2d25757","transparentToolbar":false,"campaignData":{"NewsArticleVectorId":["3acc0734-49bd-474e-ad4f-e05746738811"]}},"resources":{"0877cd4c-78d8-372d-99a6-48272e4bdb78":{"URL":"asset://74f77dfd-5033-41ac-96e5-bfa5ad36c8b3","classification":"image","dimensions":{"height":500,"width":750},"fileSize":0,"identifier":"0877cd4c-78d8-372d-99a6-48272e4bdb78","imageIdentifier":"af45279719aa61edab437fdda2d25757","type":"image"},"08a13989-5466-4074-89ed-bf7e1bc43f8d":{"URL":"asset://08a13989-5466-4074-89ed-bf7e1bc43f8d","classification":"font","fileSize":0,"fontAttributes":{"familyName":"Source Sans Pro","style":"normal","weig